{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB11978 - MONDO_17893",
  "verbalization": "Glasdegib is being considered for repurposing in the treatment of Inherited Acute Myeloid Leukemia (IAML) based on its association with Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) through a series of indication links. The evidence suggests that Glasdegib may exert a therapeutic effect on IAML by targeting the same pathway that mediates the action of Mitoxantrone, a known agent in the treatment of AML. This is supported by the shared indication links between Glasdegib and AML subtypes, including those with Npm1 somatic mutations or specific chromosomal abnormalities like Inv3(p21;q26.2) or T(3;3)(p21;q26.2). The presence of these subtypes in the indication chain implies that Glasdegib may act through a mechanism that is relevant to the pathophysiology of IAML, potentially by modulating pathways involved in leukemia cell proliferation or survival. The high scores in the final_score suggest that the mechanistic link is robust and well-supported by the evidence provided.",
